<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charter of Carnaro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Charter of Carnaro was the constitution of the Italian Regency of Carnaro, proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">Gabriele D'Annunzio</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a> in September 1920 and drafted largely by the syndicalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alceste-de-ambris">Alceste De Ambris</a>. It organised citizens into ten corporations, guaranteed broad civil rights including equality of the sexes, and declared music a fundamental institution of the state. Its corporatism influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charter-of-carnaro.jpg" alt="Charter of Carnaro" width="251" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Decius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Free_State_of_Fiume_1920-1924.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its constitution, the <b>Charter of Carnaro</b>, drafted with the syndicalist Alceste De Ambris, organised citizens into corporations and declared music a fundamental institution of the state.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-regency-of-carnaro">Italian Regency of Carnaro</a></li></ul></section>
